WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.933
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.933 --> 00:00:02.333
Thursday the twelfth.

00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:07.132
The rain day, with a full calendar under it and a late evening on there.

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:09.598
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:09.598 --> 00:00:14.930
What's the safe ceiling for true peak in a podcast file, and why leave that gap?

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:16.263
Answer's at the end.

00:00:16.263 --> 00:00:17.996
Weather's the story today.

00:00:17.996 --> 00:00:27.660
Dry this morning, then rain from late morning, heaviest early afternoon, a lighter patch around five, and steady again from seven into the night.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:33.592
It keeps going through Friday morning and turns cold, close to forty by Friday afternoon.

00:00:33.592 --> 00:00:38.458
I couldn't pull wind numbers this morning, so check that before you ride.

00:00:38.458 --> 00:00:39.391
Okay, the day.

00:00:39.391 --> 00:00:43.323
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:43.323 --> 00:00:48.389
There's a lens return on there for the morning, with the time still not set.

00:00:48.389 --> 00:00:50.255
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:50.255 --> 00:00:52.655
And plans on there at eight tonight.

00:00:52.655 --> 00:00:58.320
That five o'clock lull is your best window to get home before the rain picks back up.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:59.253
Alright, news.

00:00:59.253 --> 00:01:00.653
One from your corner.

00:01:00.653 --> 00:01:04.385
Adobe used its MAX conference this week to turn its A.I.

00:01:04.385 --> 00:01:06.985
helper into something that's always on.

00:01:06.985 --> 00:01:14.983
It watches what you're working on and jumps in on quick jobs, like cutting out a background or making a video thumbnail.

00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:26.513
The bigger part for you is a new local connector that lets chatbots like Claude, ChatGPT, and Gemini reach into Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, and After Effects directly.

00:01:26.513 --> 00:01:27.513
Closer to home.

00:01:27.513 --> 00:01:34.978
The Agriculture Department proposed a rule that would cut about six point three million people from food stamps.

00:01:34.978 --> 00:01:36.911
It's only a proposal for now.

00:01:36.911 --> 00:01:45.442
But New York has one of the biggest food stamp rolls in the country, so this one would land hard in the city if it goes through.

00:01:45.442 --> 00:01:53.107
And in Europe, at least four drones drifted into Lithuania, Latvia, and Estonia during Ukrainian strikes on Russia.

00:01:53.107 --> 00:01:56.240
People got shelter alerts, and nobody was hurt.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:05.771
It lands as NATO reportedly weighs its biggest planning overhaul in about twenty years, shifting its focus toward a possible fight with Russia.

00:02:05.771 --> 00:02:07.970
Okay, here's one for the weekend.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:15.835
A new spot called Chyelle, on Cook Street at White Street in Bushwick, is a vintage furniture shop with a cafe inside.

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:23.234
It's run by the person behind a big Instagram account about Facebook Marketplace finds, so the stuff is picked.

00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:28.166
The rooms are set up like living rooms, so you can actually sit in things.

00:02:28.166 --> 00:02:35.298
Glassware and lamps start around twenty bucks, and Danish walnut chairs run about a hundred and forty each.

00:02:35.298 --> 00:02:38.430
It's open every day but Tuesday, nine to eight.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:45.495
Worth a slow wander on a cold Saturday, and the rooms alone might give you a few ideas for props and sets.

00:02:45.495 --> 00:02:47.828
Today's challenge is for your head.

00:02:47.828 --> 00:02:56.826
Pick one wait today, a coffee line, a slow elevator, the minute before a call, and leave your phone in your pocket for the whole thing.

00:02:56.826 --> 00:02:57.959
Just look around.

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:02.691
Find three things you've walked past a hundred times and never noticed.

00:03:02.691 --> 00:03:07.223
It's the same habit that finds a shot before you go looking for one.

00:03:07.223 --> 00:03:09.690
Okay, something you've been learning.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:14.822
Remember wiring up the memo so a push renders the audio and updates the feed?

00:03:14.822 --> 00:03:23.020
Here's what actually happens on GitHub's side after you push, and three small settings in your own setup that make it work.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:25.819
First, every run gets a brand new machine.

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:31.485
GitHub rents you a fresh computer, runs your steps, then throws the whole thing away.

00:03:31.485 --> 00:03:32.884
Nothing carries over.

00:03:32.884 --> 00:03:38.416
That's why your workflow installs the audio tool from scratch every single morning.

00:03:38.416 --> 00:03:40.882
Wasteful, but every run starts clean.

00:03:40.882 --> 00:03:42.149
Second, the filter.

00:03:42.149 --> 00:03:48.281
Your setup only wakes up when something changes in a few folders, like scripts or the tools.

00:03:48.281 --> 00:03:55.146
So when the bot commits the finished audio afterward, that commit lands in a folder the filter ignores.

00:03:55.146 --> 00:04:02.144
And on top of that, GitHub won't let a push made by a workflow's own built-in key start another workflow.

00:04:02.144 --> 00:04:06.277
Two locks, so the bot can't trigger itself in an endless loop.

00:04:06.277 --> 00:04:07.343
Third, the line.

00:04:07.343 --> 00:04:12.009
Your setup puts every render in one group, and tells it not to cancel.

00:04:12.009 --> 00:04:18.474
So if two pushes land a minute apart, the second one waits its turn instead of killing the first.

00:04:18.474 --> 00:04:25.872
Then they each push to the same branch one after the other, which is why there's a pull right before that push.

00:04:25.872 --> 00:04:31.804
And the last step hands the finished folder to Pages, which serves it as a plain website.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:34.470
That's the feed your podcast app checks.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:41.335
So: calendar says run at seven, the lens return in the morning, Godinger till five, and plans at eight.

00:04:41.335 --> 00:04:43.135
Skip the phone in one line.

00:04:43.135 --> 00:04:48.000
The answer: about minus one, so nothing clips after the M.P.3 conversion.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Stay dry, Ziad.
